# IndustriQ — Version 1 Requirements

Status: Initial draft
Author: John Sullins

## 1. Purpose

IndustriQ will monitor simulated motor and VFD data, identify unusual
operating conditions, and explain the measurements behind its findings.

Version 1 will focus on one motor-driven cement-plant fan. It will establish
a foundation for additional equipment types and real data sources.

## 2. Intended User

A maintenance technician or engineer who wants to:

- Review equipment operating history.
- Identify readings that exceed configured limits.
- Compare behavior under similar operating conditions.
- Investigate unusual current or temperature trends.

## 3. Version 1 Scope

Version 1 includes:

- A Python equipment simulator
- CSV data loading and validation
- Equipment-specific configuration
- Rule-based health assessment
- Trend visualization
- A baseline machine learning model
- A simple front end

Development will proceed in stages. The simulator and analysis will work
before the front end is added.

## 4. Initial Equipment and Data

The initial asset will be a simulated motor-driven fan controlled by a VFD.

Required measurement fields:

| Field | Type | Unit / Values |
| --- | --- | --- |
| timestamp | Date/time | UTC |
| equipment_id | Text | Unique asset identifier |
| run_state | Text | STOPPED, STARTING, RUNNING, STOPPING |
| current_a | Number | Amperes |
| frequency_hz | Number | Hertz |
| motor_temperature_c | Number | Degrees Celsius |
| load_demand_pct | Number | 0–100 percent |

Motor temperature will represent a simplified winding-temperature signal.

Load demand will be a simulated process-demand input. It will not be
presented as measured torque or VFD thermal-model percentage.

## 5. Functional Requirements

### FR-01 — Equipment Configuration

The system shall store the following configuration for each asset:

- Equipment ID and display name
- Rated motor current
- Maximum operating frequency
- Current warning and critical limits
- Temperature warning and critical limits
- Alert persistence duration

Warning limits shall be lower than critical limits.

Configuration values are demonstration settings and shall not be
represented as manufacturer-approved protection settings.

### FR-02 — Normal-Operation Simulation

The simulator shall generate a chronological sequence containing:

- Stopped periods
- Starts and stops
- Steady operation
- Changes in frequency and load demand
- Small measurement variations
- Gradual heating and cooling

Current shall respond to operating conditions. Temperature shall change
gradually rather than instantly following current.

The simulation assumptions shall be documented.

### FR-03 — Reproducible Simulation

The simulator shall accept:

- Equipment configuration
- Start timestamp
- Duration
- Sampling interval
- Random seed

The same inputs and seed shall reproduce the same dataset.

### FR-04 — Abnormal Scenarios

The simulator shall support independently scheduled scenarios:

- Increased mechanical resistance
- Reduced cooling
- Missing sensor readings
- Sensor spikes
- Stuck sensor readings

Each scenario shall have a defined start time, duration, and severity
where applicable.

### FR-05 — Separate Evaluation Labels

The simulator shall save injected scenario labels separately from
measurement data.

Labels shall identify the affected timestamps and scenario type.

Scenario labels shall not be used as model input features.

### FR-06 — CSV Loading

The system shall load measurement data from CSV.

It shall parse timestamps, identify equipment, and arrange readings
chronologically for each asset.

Loading shall not modify the original file.

### FR-07 — Data Validation

The system shall detect and report:

- Missing required columns
- Invalid timestamps
- Non-numeric measurement values
- Missing readings
- Unknown equipment IDs
- Unsupported run states
- Duplicate equipment/timestamp pairs
- Values outside documented valid ranges

Invalid readings shall not silently become zero or be classified as normal.

Validation results shall identify the affected field and row.

### FR-08 — Rule-Based Assessment

The system shall assess valid readings using equipment-specific limits.

Equipment condition shall be one of:

- NORMAL
- WARNING
- CRITICAL
- UNKNOWN

Operating state shall be displayed separately from equipment condition.

A stopped asset shall not automatically be considered healthy. For example,
a stopped motor may still have a high temperature.

Missing or invalid required measurements shall produce UNKNOWN when a
reliable assessment cannot be made.

### FR-09 — Alert Persistence and Explanation

The system shall support a configurable persistence duration for
measurement-limit alerts.

A reading shall trigger a persistent alert only after the configured
duration is satisfied by consecutive valid readings. Data gaps shall
interrupt that sequence.

Each alert shall include:

- Equipment ID
- Affected signal
- Observed value
- Applicable limit
- Start timestamp
- Duration
- Human-readable explanation

Rule-based alerts and ML findings shall be identified separately.

### FR-10 — Trend Visualization

The system shall plot:

- Motor current
- VFD output frequency
- Motor temperature
- Load demand

Plots shall include timestamps and units.

Users shall be able to select an equipment asset and time range.

### FR-11 — Baseline Machine Learning Model

The first model shall estimate expected motor current from operating
conditions such as frequency and load demand.

Initial training shall use designated normal RUNNING periods.

The system shall calculate the difference between observed and predicted
current and identify sustained unusual deviations.

The model shall flag unusual behavior without claiming a confirmed
mechanical fault.

### FR-12 — Model Evaluation

Training, validation, and test data shall be separated by simulation run
or chronological period.

Randomly mixing adjacent readings across these sets shall be avoided.

Preprocessing shall be fitted using training data only.

Regression evaluation shall include MAE and RMSE.

Anomaly evaluation shall include precision, recall, and false alerts on
normal operation, using the separate scenario labels.

Results shall document which scenarios and operating conditions were tested.

### FR-13 — Front End

The front end shall display:

- Equipment name
- Timestamp of the displayed reading
- Operating state
- Rule-based condition
- Latest measurement values and units
- Alert explanations
- Trend plots
- ML predictions and anomaly findings, when available

Historical or simulated data shall be clearly identified.

## 6. Nonfunctional Requirements

### NFR-01 — Understandable Structure

Simulation, data validation, health rules, model training, and presentation
shall be separated into modules.

### NFR-02 — Reproducibility

Dataset generation settings and model evaluation settings shall be retained
so results can be reproduced.

### NFR-03 — Traceability

The project shall use Git to track incremental development.

The repository shall document setup instructions, assumptions, and completed
milestones.

### NFR-04 — Verification

Checks shall cover meaningful behavior, including:

- Reproducible simulation
- Invalid-data handling
- Equipment-specific limits
- Alert persistence
- Separation of evaluation labels from model inputs

### NFR-05 — Local Operation

Version 1 shall run locally without requiring a plant connection or paid
cloud service.

### NFR-06 — Integration Readiness

The analysis engine shall operate independently of the simulator,
data source, and dashboard.

It shall accept a documented input format and return structured
results so future historian connectors and APIs can reuse the
same analysis.

## 7. Out of Scope for Version 1

- PLC control or automatic equipment shutdown
- Live historian integration
- High-frequency current waveform analysis
- Vibration FFT and bearing-frequency analysis
- Confirmed fault diagnosis
- Remaining-useful-life predictions
- Production deployment
- User accounts and access management

## 8. Completion Criteria

Version 1 is complete when a user can:

1. Generate a reproducible dataset for the example fan.
2. Load it and review data-quality findings.
3. View operating state and explained rule-based alerts.
4. Explore measurement trends.
5. Train and evaluate the baseline current model.
6. Compare model findings with injected scenarios.
7. View the results through a simple front end.

## 9. First Development Milestone

The first milestone includes only:

- Repository structure
- Equipment configuration
- Normal-operation simulator
- CSV output
- Basic plots for inspecting generated behavior

Abnormal scenarios, health rules, ML, and the front end will follow after
normal operation has been reviewed.
